\fs32\b Ruler\b0\fs20\par
\par
The ruler helps you follow individual lines across a scan and makes it easier to keep your place while transcribing or verifying entries.\par
\par
\fs24\b Showing the ruler\b0\fs20\par
Use the Ruler command to display the ruler over the scan.\par
\par
The ruler can be positioned so that it lines up with the rows of entries on the scanned page.\par
\par
\fs24\b Setting the ruler position\b0\fs20\par
To set up the ruler accurately, first position it on the first line of the index and then set the position of the tenth line.\par
\par
WinBMD2 uses these two positions to calculate the spacing between the lines on the scan.\par
\par
Once the ruler has been set, it can move from one index line to the next while keeping the correct spacing.\par
\par
\fs24\b Adjusting the ruler\b0\fs20\par
If the ruler is not aligned correctly with the entries, reposition it and reset the reference lines as necessary.\par
\par
Different scans may require different ruler positions because the spacing and alignment of the original images can vary.\par
\par
\fs24\b Remembering ruler settings\b0\fs20\par
WinBMD2 remembers ruler settings so that suitable positioning can be reused when working with scans of the same type.\par
\par
\fs24\b Using the ruler while transcribing\b0\fs20\par
Position the ruler over the entry currently being transcribed. It provides a visual guide across the page and can make it easier to follow a row when the scan contains many closely spaced entries.\par
\par
The ruler can also be useful during verification when comparing the verification boxes with the corresponding line on the scan.\par
